let's get into audience questions for  the week first question hey David  isn't it time to end daylight savings  time once and for all this is an  interesting question we talk about it  every year when we do the time change in  the fall and then in the spring one  thing is that I'm gonna address the  question but usually when people talk  about should we end daylight savings  time very often they actually mean  should we stay on daylight savings time  all year round in other words let's not  go back to standard time back and forth  so this goes back to 1918 and the idea  of daylight savings time to begin with  was to conserve energy the idea was if  in the winter when you wake up it's dark  you're going to use more energy you are  going to it's going to be colder because  the Sun is not yet up at all so you're  gonna use more heat you're gonna use  more lights so on and so forth in  reality I believe that going that ending  daylight savings time causes more  problems than it probably solves so the  energy savings number one are completely  unproven the light savings when you  consider that light bulbs particularly  as we switch to the more  energy-efficient ones are not actually  responsible for that much of all energy  usage can be outweighed by AC usage or  using more heat when people are home and  it is already darker out for example in  some places it really depends where you  live because the United States has so  many different climates and then in  addition to that you do have to account  for the number of car accidents that  take place for two reasons one is just  the switch is a sort of jet lag it's  only an hour but every time you move  forward and back you either have people  staying up an extra hour on Saturday  night because they know that they're  getting that hour back for free so to  speak to sleep that can increase car  accidents lead to people having one more  drink before driving home and then in  general when you move driving at night  when you have more driving at night as a  result of the fact that we're moving the  clocks so that it's the Sun comes up an  hour earlier but  sunsets an hour earlier you do have more  people driving home in the dark and that  would correlate with an increased number  of car accidents so the the benefit of  the benefits that daylight savings times  end is supposed to bring are  questionable at best and possibly  completely counterproductive in reality  so there are people who talk about  canceled daylight savings time I  actually think what we want to cancel is  the switching back and forth and I would  say let's let's make it permanent let's  make daylight savings time permanent the  way to do it is probably going to come  federally so States and some states do  opt out of changing time the way that  most states do I think air part or all  of Arizona part or all of Indiana do  this and of course individual states can  do that but I think really the way to do  this is the federal government should  just say we're not going to move the  clocks forward and back anymore we are  going to just stay my preference would  be on daylight savings time all the time  and then states and time zones would  follow there there isn't one other idea  which is let's reduce the number of time  zones and since it's not really the  question I don't want to get into the  details of how that you might think well  ok that might make it easier so that  there aren't as many hours separating  the East Coast and the west coast but  how does that relate to the question of  the changing of the clocks in daylight  savings time versus Standard Time that's  a story for another day but that is  another idea that that people sometimes  present so I am an advocate of ending  the time switching but I don't want to  end daylight savings time I want to make  daylight savings time permanent I really  don't care if when I get up in the  morning it's still sort of dark or  depending on what time you get out  completely dark I would love that extra  hour of sunlight and I would actually  argue that the health benefits of that  people are way more likely to go to the  gym after work or to do something social  after work if it's not pitch black at  4:45 in the middle of December in  Massachusetts and so I think that that's  the direction we should go
 

